\item \( \frac{3}{4} \times 12 = \_\_\_\_\_\_ \)
\end{enumerate}
<<18>> Give me a combination of an investigation and a partner activity for a 1st grade student learning science. It should have a subsection for materials. It should be an exercise on making things go faster or slower.
<<18>> \begin{enumerate}
  \item Talk with your partner about how to make things go faster or slower.
  \item Choose one thing that goes fast and one thing that goes slow.
  \item Write the words ‘faster’ and ‘slower’ on sticky notes.
  \item Stick the words on the things you have chosen.
  \item Share your words and things with the class.
  \item Collect the words and things from your classmates.
  \item Put the words in two groups: faster and slower.
  \item Look at the things in each group.
  \item Talk with your partner about what you notice.
  \item Share your ideas with the class.
  \item Try to answer the questions: What makes things go faster or slower?
\end{enumerate}

\subsection*{Materials}
\begin{itemize}
  \item Sticky notes
  \item Various items for demonstration
\end{itemize}
<<19>> Give me a combination of an investigation exercise, a partner activity, and a question for a 2nd grade student learning science. It should have a subsection for materials. It should be an exercise on light sources.
<<19>> \begin{itemize}
\item Work with a partner. Sit facing each other. Both of you hold up a light source.
\item Try to see the light sources. What do you notice? Can you see each other's light sources?
\item Look at your partner's light source. Can you see it?
\item Look at your own light source. Can you see it?
\item Share your results with the class.
\item Try to explain what you have observed.
\end{itemize}

\subsection*{Materials}
\begin{itemize}
\item Light sources (e.g., flashlights or lanterns)
\end{itemize}
