<<77>> Give me a combination of a word problem and an arithmetic exercise for a 5th grade student learning mathematics. It should be an exercise on sharing. The exercise should contain some kind of a graphic component (illustration, diagram, table, etc).
<<77>> Share 18 balloons equally between 4 children. How many balloons does each child get? Draw the balloons to show how they are shared.

[STRDGM] 18 balloons. [STPDGM]
<<78>> Give me a combination of an arithmetic exercise and a word problem for a 5th grade student learning mathematics. It should be an exercise on proportions. It should have multiple steps.
<<78>> The proportion of men at a party was \( \frac{3}{8} \) and the proportion of women was \( \frac{5}{8} \). If 15 more men arrived, what was the new proportion of men?

\begin{enumerate}[label=\alph*.]
\item How many men were there initially based on the proportion \( \frac{3}{8} \)?
\item How many women were there initially based on the proportion \( \frac{5}{8} \)?
\item What is the new proportion of men after 15 more men arrive?
\end{enumerate}
<<79>> Give me a combination of an arithmetic exercise and a word problem for a 2nd grade student learning mathematics. It should be an exercise involving jumping. The exercise should contain some kind of a graphic component (illustration, diagram, table, etc). It should have multiple steps.
<<79>> Jump 2 spaces from 0. Write the missing numbers.

\begin{enumerate}[label=\alph*.]
\item What number do you land on after the first jump?
\item What number do you land on after the second jump?
\end{enumerate}

