<<111>> Give me a comparison exercise for a 4th grade student learning mathematics. The exercise should contain some kind of a graphic component (illustration, diagram, table, etc). It should be an exercise on angles. It should have multiple steps.
<<111>> Compare the angles in each of the following shapes and list them in order from smallest to greatest:

\begin{enumerate}[label=\alph*.]
\item Angles A, B, C, and D in a rectangle
\item Angles E, F, G, and H in a square
\item Angles I, J, K, and L in a triangle
\item Angles M, N, O, and P in a circle
\end{enumerate}

[STRDGM] A rectangle labeled with angles A, B, C, and D. [STPDGM]
[STRDGM] A square labeled with angles E, F, G, and H. [STPDGM]
[STRDGM] A triangle labeled with angles I, J, and K. [STPDGM]
[STRDGM] A circle labeled with angles M, N, and O. [STPDGM]
<<112>> Give me a true-false exercise for a 5th grade student learning science. It should be an exercise on light. It should be a with Answer.
<<112>> When light passes from air into a denser material, it slows down. True / False

\subsection*{Answers}
\begin{itemize}
\item True
\end{itemize}
