<<175>> Give me a combination of a word problem and an arithmetic exercise for a 6th grade student learning mathematics. It should be an exercise involving ratios. It should have multiple steps.
<<175>> The ratio of the number of pupils in School A to the number of pupils in School B was \( 5 : 3 \). After some pupils from School A moved to School B, the ratio became \( 7 : 4 \). If 140 more pupils moved from School A to School B, what was the new ratio of the number of pupils in School A to the number of pupils in School B?

\begin{enumerate}[label=\alph*.]
\item Let \( x \) be the initial number of pupils in School A and \( y \) be the initial number of pupils in School B. Express the initial ratio of pupils in School A to pupils in School B in terms of \( x \) and \( y \).
\item Use the information that the ratio became \( 7 : 4 \) after some pupils moved from School A to School B to set up an equation involving \( x \) and \( y \).
\item Solve for \( x \) and \( y \) using the equation from step 2.
\item Find the new ratio of the number of pupils in School A to the number of pupils in School B after 140 more pupils moved from School A to School B.
\end{enumerate}
<<176>> Give me an arithmetic exercise for a 4th grade student learning mathematics. It should be an exercise involving number lines. It should have multiple steps.
<<176>> Draw the following on a number line: -3, -2, -1, 0, 1, 2, 3. Write the missing numbers.

\begin{enumerate}[label=\alph*.]
\item 3 more than -2
\item 2 more than -3
\item 2 less than -1
\item 1 less than 0
\end{enumerate}
