<<382>> Give me a word problem for a 3rd grade student learning mathematics. It should have a subsection for answers. It should have multiple steps.
<<382>> A box contains 12 biscuits. 5 biscuits are eaten.

\begin{enumerate}[label=\alph*.]
\item How many biscuits are left?
\item If 3 more biscuits are eaten, how many will be left?
\end{enumerate}

\subsection*{Answers}
\begin{enumerate}[label=\alph*.]
\item There are 7 biscuits left.
\item There will be 4 biscuits left.
\end{enumerate}
<<383>> Give me a combination of an arithmetic exercise and a word problem for a 6th grade student learning mathematics. It should have multiple steps. It should be a with Answer.
<<383>> The ratio of the number of students in Class A to the number of students in Class B was \( 5 : 7 \). There were 50 more students in Class B than in Class A. Later, 20 students from Class B joined Class A.

\begin{enumerate}[label=\alph*.]
\item How many students were initially in Class A and Class B?
\item What is the new ratio of the number of students in Class A to the number of students in Class B after the 20 students join?
\end{enumerate}

\subsection*{Answers}
\begin{enumerate}[label=\alph*.]
\item Let \( x \) be the number of students in Class A and \( 1.4x \) be the number of students in Class B. We have the equation \( 1.4x = x + 50 \). Solving for \( x \), we get \( 0.4x = 50 \), which gives \( x = 125 \). So, there are initially 125 students in Class A and 175 students in Class B.
\item After 20 students from Class B join Class A, Class A will have \( 125 + 20 = 145 \) students, and Class B will have \( 175 - 20 = 155 \) students. The new ratio of the number of students in Class A to the number of students in Class B is \( \frac{145}{155} \), which simplifies to \( \frac{29}{31} \).
\end{enumerate}
<<384>> Give me a combination of an arithmetic exercise and a word problem for a 2nd grade student learning mathematics. It should have multiple steps. It should be a with Answer.
<<384>> How many times can you fit a 1-litre bottle into a 2-litre bottle?

How many times can you fit a 2-litre bottle into a 3-litre bottle?

How many times can you fit a 3-litre bottle into a 5-litre bottle?

\subsection*{Answers}
You can fit a 1-litre bottle into a 2-litre bottle 2 times.

You can fit a 2-litre bottle into a 3-litre bottle 1.5 times.

You can fit a 3-litre bottle into a 5-litre bottle 1.666... times.
