<<510>> Give me a combination of a short answer exercise and a thought exercise for a 3rd grade student learning computer science.
<<510>> Imagine you are a shopkeeper. You have a list of the items you sell.  
\begin{enumerate}[label=\alph*.]
\item How do you arrange the items on the list? 
\item How would you find a particular item on the list? 
\end{enumerate}
<<511>> Give me an arithmetic exercise for a 4th grade student learning mathematics.
<<511>> Write a 5-digit number that has 2 in the ones place, 5 in the tens place, and 7 in the hundreds place. Then write the number in words. The number is \_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_ and it is written in words as \_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_.
<<512>> Give me an arithmetic exercise for a 1st grade student learning mathematics.
<<512>> Find the sum: \( 3 + 2 = \) \_\_\_\_\_\_
<<513>> Give me an arithmetic exercise for a 3rd grade student learning mathematics.
<<513>> Write the missing number:
\begin{equation*}
3 \times 7 = \_\_\_
\end{equation*}
<<514>> Give me a combination of an arithmetic exercise, a fill in the blank exercise, and a word problem for a 1st grade student learning mathematics.
<<514>> There are 12 boys in a class. 4 of them are playing soccer. How many boys are not playing soccer?

\begin{equation*}
12 - 4 = \_\_\_
\end{equation*}

There are \_\_\_ boys not playing soccer.
<<515>> Give me a combination of a word problem and an arithmetic exercise for a 3rd grade student learning mathematics.
<<515>> Mr. Chen had 900 stamps. He gave 200 stamps to each of his 3 sons. How many stamps did he have left? How many stamps did he give away altogether? Show your work. Write the missing numbers. He had \_\_\_\_\_\_ stamps left. He gave away \_\_\_\_\_\_ stamps altogether.
