<<596>> Give me an arithmetic exercise for a 1st grade student learning mathematics. It should be an exercise involving number sentences. The exercise should contain some kind of a graphic component (illustration, diagram, table, etc). It should have multiple steps.
<<596>> Write 2 number sentences for each picture.

\begin{enumerate}[label=\alph*.]
\item 2 fish in each bowl.
\item 3 bowls.
\item 2 + 2 = 4
\item 2 + 2 = 4
\item 2 + 4 = 6
\item 6 - 2 = 4
\end{enumerate}

[STRDGM] Two bowls each containing 2 fish. [STPDGM]
[STRDGM] Two bowls each containing 3 fish. [STPDGM]
[STRDGM] Two bowls each containing 4 fish. [STPDGM]
<<597>> Give me an arithmetic exercise for a 3rd grade student learning mathematics. It should be an exercise involving fractions. It should be an exercise involving number lines. The exercise should contain some kind of a graphic component (illustration, diagram, table, etc). It should have multiple steps.
<<597>> Draw a ring around the equivalent fractions on the number line below.

\begin{enumerate}[label=\alph*.]
\item Identify the fractions that are equivalent to \( \frac{1}{2} \).
\item Identify the fractions that are equivalent to \( \frac{1}{4} \).
\item Identify the fractions that are equivalent to \( \frac{3}{4} \).
\end{enumerate}

[STRDGM] A number line from 0 to 1 with tick marks at 0.25, 0.5, 0.75, 1, and corresponding fractions \( \frac{1}{4} \), \( \frac{1}{2} \), \( \frac{3}{4} \), and 1. [STPDGM]
<<598>> Give me an arithmetic exercise for a 3rd grade student learning mathematics. It should be an exercise on rounding. It should have multiple steps.
<<598>> Round each number to the nearest ten:

\begin{enumerate}[label=\alph*.]
\item 23
\item 37
\item 56
\item 69
\item 81
\item 93
\end{enumerate}
<<599>> Give me a combination of a group activity and a drawing exercise for a 2nd grade student learning science. It should have a subsection for materials. It should be an exercise on shadows.
<<599>> \begin{itemize}
\item Go outside with your group and find a sunny spot.
\item Look for shadows. Choose a shadow to investigate.
\item Draw around the shadow to show its shape and size.
\item Move your shadow puppet so it is in a different place. Observe what happens to the shadow.
\item Share your results with the class.
\end{itemize}

\subsection*{Materials}
\begin{itemize}
\item Paper
\item Pencils
\end{itemize}
<<600>> Give me a question answer exercise for a student learning Dutch. It should be an exercise involving an example.
<<600>> Answer these questions, following the example.
\begin{enumerate}
\item Hoeveel dagen in de week?\\
Zeven dagen.
\item Hoeveel maanden in het jaar?\\
