<<671>> Give me a combination of an identification exercise and a geometry exercise for a 4th grade student learning mathematics. It should have multiple steps.
<<671>> Identify the nets of the following shapes:

\begin{enumerate}[label=\alph*.]
\item A cube
\item A cuboid
\item A triangular prism
\item A square pyramid
\item A triangular pyramid
\end{enumerate}
<<672>> Give me a combination of a probability exercise and a group activity for a 4th grade student learning mathematics. It should have multiple steps.
<<672>> Work in a group of four.

\begin{enumerate}[label=\alph*.]
\item Choose a number on a dice that is greater than 4.
\item Roll the dice 10 times and record the outcomes.
\item Calculate the probability of rolling the chosen number.
\item Share your results with the class and compare them with the probabilities of other groups.
\end{enumerate}
<<673>> Give me a combination of a data interpretation exercise and a word problem for a 3rd grade student learning mathematics. The exercise should contain some kind of a graphic component (illustration, diagram, table, etc). It should have multiple steps.
<<673>> How many people like each fruit? Write the total number of people in the boxes.

\begin{enumerate}[label=\alph*.]
\item How many people like apples?
\item How many people like bananas?
\item How many people like oranges?
\item How many people like grapes?
\end{enumerate}

[STRDGM] A table with columns for "Fruits" and "Number of People". The "Fruits" column lists the following: Apples, Bananas, Oranges, and Grapes. The "Number of People" column shows the corresponding numbers for each fruit. [STPDGM]
<<674>> Give me a combination of an arithmetic exercise and a fill in the blank exercise for a 1st grade student learning mathematics. The exercise should contain some kind of a graphic component (illustration, diagram, table, etc). It should have multiple steps.
<<674>> How many pairs of socks are there?

\begin{enumerate}[label=\alph*.]
\item There are \_\_\_ pairs of socks.
\item There are \_\_\_ socks altogether.
\end{enumerate}

[STRDGM] 5 pairs of socks, each pair consisting of two socks. [STPDGM]
<<675>> Give me a combination of a measurement exercise and a sorting exercise for a 1st grade student learning mathematics. The exercise should contain some kind of a graphic component (illustration, diagram, table, etc). It should have multiple steps.
<<675>> Choose the shortest object and the longest object from each set. Write the number of each object.

\begin{enumerate}[label=\alph*.]
\item List the shortest and longest objects in the first row.
\item List the shortest and longest objects in the second row.
\item List the shortest and longest objects in the third row.
\end{enumerate}

[STRDGM] A row of three objects labeled A,B, and C, with A being the shortest and C being the longest. [STPDGM]
[STRDGM] A row of three objects labeled D, E, and F, with D being the shortest and F being the longest. [STPDGM]
[STRDGM] A row of three objects labeled G, H, and I, with G being the shortest and I being the longest. [STPDGM]
<<676>> Give me a combination of a short answer exercise and a fill in the blank exercise for a 1st grade student learning mathematics. The exercise should contain some kind of a graphic component (illustration, diagram, table, etc). It should have multiple steps.
