<<851>> Give me a combination of a drawing exercise and an activity for a 1st grade student learning computer science.
<<851>> Draw a picture of a computer. Draw a circle around the things you can touch. Draw a cross through the things you can't touch. Now, show your picture to the class. Tell them which parts of the computer they can touch and which parts they can't. Can you think of a way to tell the class without touching the computer? How would you do that? Think about how you can use your voice or gestures to convey the information. Try it out and see how it works! Describe the actions you would take to explain the parts of the computer without touching it. For example, you could point to the screen, keyboard, and mouse, and explain what each part does. You could also use gestures to show how to interact with the computer, like mimicking typing on the keyboard or moving the mouse. How would you use your voice to explain the different parts and their functions? Think about how you can clearly describe each component and its purpose without touching the computer. Share your ideas with the class and discuss how you can effectively communicate the information without physical interaction. Remember to use simple language and focus on the key parts of the computer that you can touch or not touch. By explaining your approach, you'll help others understand how to communicate about computers in a way that's easy to follow. Give it a try! Draw a picture of a computer and label the parts you can touch and the parts you can't. Use your voice and gestures to explain the different components and their functions. Share your drawing and explanation with the class and discuss how you can effectively communicate the information without physical interaction.
